Example 14 -- the Mona-Lisa problem (GPU-accelerated)
=====================================================

Goal
----
Evolve a set of alpha-blended circle-triangles whose superimposition resembles a target image. Each
candidate is a GImageIndividual: N triangles (10 parameters each: centre, radius, three angles, RGB,
alpha) plus a background colour. The fitness is the per-pixel, per-channel deviation of the rendered
candidate from the target image, passed through a rational saturation function -- the smaller, the
closer the resemblance.

What is new
-----------
This example is evaluated through the unified courtier GPU consumer
(Gem::Courtier::GPU::GGPUConsumerT), a first-class, mnemonic-selectable consumer (folded into
gemfony-courtier when GENEVA_BUILD_WITH_GPU_CONSUMER is ON): run with `--consumer gpu` to evaluate on
the device, or with any other consumer (e.g. the default `--consumer stc`) to evaluate on the CPU through
the individual's own evaluate(). A whole generation is flattened and scored in ONE bulk kernel launch.
The kernel (kernels/monalisa_eval_{double,float}.cu) is compiled at RUN TIME (NVRTC), so:

  * the example builds with an ordinary C++ toolchain -- no CUDA language is enabled at build time;
  * the kernel is chosen in config/GGPUConsumer.json, so you can switch it without recompiling.

The kernel is pixel-parallel (256 threads cooperate on each candidate and atomic-accumulate its
fitness), so even a modest population fills the GPU. The SAME render+score math is also implemented on
the CPU (GImageIndividual::evaluate() and the shared host renderer in GMonaLisaProblem.hpp), so a CPU
run cross-checks the GPU result (see the parity-check mode below).

Because there is no network involved, this example doubles as a convenient stress-test harness for the
courtier consumer framework and Hap.

Structure: a loadable module + a thin launcher
----------------------------------------------
The optimization individual (GImageIndividual) AND its GPU marshaller (GMonaLisaGPUMarshaller) are
packaged into ONE runtime-loadable shared object, libGImageIndividual.so (built by
GImageIndividualPlugin.cpp, a two-contribution manifest -- the same GPU-problem-as-a-module shape as
example 21). GCUDAWorker is a thin launcher: it loads that module with `--module`, names only the
flat-genome base and the loaded factory, and adds the two pieces a generic optimizer cannot -- it loads
the target image (the fitness reference + canvas) and registers GImagePOM (the per-iteration PNG output).

Building
--------
The example is built automatically when libpng is available (it is skipped otherwise) and the GPU
consumer is enabled. The launcher and the module share the compile-time scalar (float by default, or
double when GIMAGE_USE_FLOAT is OFF).

Running
-------
  ./GCUDAWorker --module ./libGImageIndividual.so --consumer gpu   # evaluate on the device
  ./GCUDAWorker --module ./libGImageIndividual.so --consumer stc   # CPU cross-check (individual's evaluate())

The GPU consumer is device-only, so a CPU run uses a CPU consumer (e.g. --consumer stc), evaluating
through the individual's own evaluate() rather than the device kernel. (The "backend" key in
config/GGPUConsumer.json only accepts "cuda".)

Example-specific settings are config-file keys in config/GImageGeneral.json (edit + re-run, no rebuild):
  target_image     The target image PNG (default ./pictures/ml-small.png); also sets the canvas size
  gpu_config       The GPU consumer config (backend + kernel selection)
  log_images       Write the best candidate image per iteration (default true)
  emit_best_only   Only emit an image when the best result improved (default true)
  parity_check_n   If > 0: run a GPU/CPU parity check over this many random individuals and exit

Output
------
The best candidate of each iteration is rasterised and written to ./results/ as
<iteration>_<fitness>_bestIndividual.png, so the picture can be watched converging towards the target.

Configuration files (config/)
-----------------------------
  Go2.json                   top-level Go2 settings
  GEvolutionaryAlgorithm.json the evolutionary algorithm (population size, iterations, ...)
  GImageIndividual.json      the image individual (number of triangles, adaption, ...)
  GGPUConsumer.json          GPU consumer: backend (cpu|cuda), kernel path/entry, launch sizes
